;; A yield point inside a loop that does *not* become a loop.
;;
;; This is the case that decides where the colour check has to live. `go` below
;; is written exactly like the named `let` in `074_bjoroutine.bjo`, which is allowed to
;; suspend — but it is also *passed to* `apply2`, and a thing you pass has to be
;; a value, so it cannot be a jump target. Loop lowering leaves it as a C# local
;; function.
;;
;; A body-local `(defun ...)` may suspend now — see `158_local_functions.bjo` —
;; and this is the same emitted shape wanting the same treatment. It has not had
;; it: a loop member is not a function-shaped binding, has no arrow of its own
;; to carry a colour, and is emitted by a different pair of emitters. So this
;; stays refused, and the message stays about the escape rather than about the
;; colour: the fix it names — stop passing `go` as a value — turns this into a
;; `while` in the enclosing bjoroutine, where a yield point was always fine.
;;
;; Two syntactically identical forms, two different answers, and nothing at type
;; checking time can tell them apart. So the check runs after `LoopLowering` and
;; asks it the same question the emitter asks — `isInlinedLoop` — rather than
;; guessing. Guessing permissively puts this program's failure in generated C#;
;; guessing conservatively rejects `sum-doubled`, which is the one workaround
;; §3.1 offers for the higher-order restriction.

;; EXPECT-ERROR: calling 'fetch' is a yield point
;; EXPECT-ERROR: yield point is not allowed here
;; EXPECT-ERROR: 'go' is a loop, but it still mentions its own name
;; EXPECT-ERROR: Stop using 'go' as a value and it becomes one

(: fetch (-> int int))
(defbjo (fetch x) (* x 2))

(: apply2 (-> (-> int int) int int))
(defun (apply2 f x) (f (f x)))

(: escaping (-> int int))
(defbjo (escaping n)
  (let go ((i 0))
    (if (> i n) (fetch i) (apply2 go (+ i 1)))))

(defun (main args) 0)
